  "interpretation": "Sample verified: two terminal fresh-rootfs read-only runs and eight fixed-input calls. Actual VM call +0x16aa48 invokes wrapper +0x263504 and descriptor target +0x25874c. The descriptor supplies a twelve-byte string and flag zero, selecting raw MD5 output. The returned sixteen bytes equal an independent Java MD5 digest and the configuration AES key. At return and the later cipher entry, the data pointer, per-byte write-event identities and snapshots match with verified temporal order. Fresh relocated ELF pages and explicit fixture allocation services run the existing vm9_objects.construct_digest_reference MD5 owner. Its GENERATED digest ACTUALLY supplies the existing configuration decrypt callback. The captured AES key is comparison-only. Generated plaintext, its RC4 slice, the generated SHA-1 RC4 key, Protobuf message, selected field, decoded AES material and native ciphertext all still match. The complete prior relation ledger is preserved. Changed or reversed MD5 input, hexadecimal output, MD5 of the decoder four-byte prefix, and MD5 of the SHA-1 preimage fail key comparisons. The MD5 and SHA-1 preimages have equal lengths but differ in each aligned four-byte piece. The MD5 input currently shows a four-byte memcpy writer and eight-byte memmove writer; their earlier copy lineage remains OPEN. Outside-module BCP labels and missing copy joins do not establish zeros, constants or absent copies. The twelve MD5 input bytes, complete SHA-1 preimage, reduction leaves, lookup key and earlier ciphertext source remain runtime inputs. Explicit fixture services do not establish Android allocator or singleton equivalence. No operand bytes, keys/IVs, plaintext, reusable material, runtime pointers or operand digests are published. Other inputs, a complete standalone signer and ordinary Cronet six-header online acceptance remain unverified."
